Overview:
  Notification Dispatch is the pattern for sending outbound messages through
  configured channels. It separates message creation (template + data) from
  delivery (provider), supports daily limits per integration, handles retries,
  and logs all delivery attempts. Notifications can be sent synchronously
  (Slack webhook) or asynchronously (email via RabbitMQ).

Rules:

  [NT1] Every notification MUST have a template and a data payload — no
        hardcoded message bodies in handlers.

  [NT2] Template rendering MUST replace placeholders with actual data values,
        falling back to default values for missing fields.

  [NT3] Provider selection MUST be configurable per integration — not hardcoded
        in business logic.

  [NT4] Daily limits MUST be enforced before sending — skip and log, never
        throw on limit exceeded.

  [NT5] Failed notifications MUST be retried with bounded retry policy (max
        attempts, backoff, dead-letter).

  [NT6] Every send attempt MUST be logged: integration_id, recipient, channel,
        provider_response, execution_time_ms.

  [NT7] Notification content MUST be sanitized (HTML escape) to prevent
        injection attacks.

  [NT8] Sensitive data in templates MUST be masked or encrypted per integration
        settings.

  [NT9] The notification service MUST be independently testable with mocked
        providers.

  [NT10] Channel fallback MUST be supported: if primary channel fails, try
         secondary channel (configurable).

Architecture:

  NotificationService:
    owns:
      - Template resolution and rendering
      - Provider abstraction (email, sms, slack, telegram)
      - Daily limit enforcement
      - Retry logic for failed sends
      - Delivery logging

    api:
      - send(integration, payload) -> {success, log_id, error}
      - queue(integration, payload) -> {queued, request_id}
      - retry(logId) -> {success, error}
      - renderTemplate(templateName, data) -> renderedContent

Components:

  NotificationProvider:
    interface:
      - send(recipient, subject, content) -> {success, provider_response}
      - validateRecipient(recipient) -> bool
    implementations:
      - MailgunProvider (email via Mailgun API)
      - SmtpProvider (email via SMTP)
      - SlackProvider (webhook-based rich messages)
      - TelegramProvider (bot API)
      - SmsProvider (SMS gateway)

  TemplateEngine:
    - render(template, data) -> renderedContent
    - replacePlaceholders(text, data) -> text
    - formatFieldValue(field, value) -> formattedValue
    placeholder_format: {{field_name}}

  DailyLimitTracker:
    - checkLimit(integrationId, date) -> {allowed, remaining, resetAt}
    - increment(integrationId, date) -> newCount
    - reset(integrationId, date) -> void

  NotificationLog:
    - integration_id, channel, recipient
    - subject, content (request body)
    - provider_response, execution_time_ms
    - is_successful, error_message
    - retry_count, sent_at

Workflow:

  send:
    1. Resolve template config from integration settings
    2. Check daily limit for integration
    3. Render template with payload data
    4. Select provider based on channel configuration
    5. Execute provider.send()
    6. Log result to notification_log
    7. Return {success, log_id}

  queue:
    1. Validate integration is mail-type (or async-enabled)
    2. Create notification log with status = queued
    3. Publish message to async queue (RabbitMQ)
    4. Return {queued, request_id}

  retry:
    1. Load notification log by ID
    2. Check canRetry() (retry_count < max_retries)
    3. Increment retry_count
    4. Re-execute provider.send()
    5. Update notification log with result

Template System:

  template_types:
    - html_template: full HTML email with placeholders
    - text_template: plain text fallback
    - slack_template: Slack message blocks with placeholders
    - sms_template: short text with placeholders

  placeholder_resolution:
    format: {{field_name}}
    sources:
      - payload fields (direct)
      - quiz_params fields (first_name, phone, fin, etc.)
      - quiz_redirect fields (amount, duration, monthly_income, etc.)
      - computed fields (timestamp, full_name, document_number)

  example:
    template: "Dear {{first_name}}, your application for {{loan_amount}} AZN is {{status}}."
    data: {first_name: "John", loan_amount: "5000", status: "approved"}
    result: "Dear John, your application for 5000 AZN is approved."

Provider Abstraction:

  EmailProvider:
    - send(to, subject, html, text) -> {success, message_id}
    - implementations: MailgunProvider, SmtpProvider

  SlackProvider:
    - send(webhook_url, message) -> {success, status_code}
    - builds: text, channel, username, icon_emoji, blocks

  TelegramProvider:
    - send(chat_id, text, parse_mode) -> {success, message_id}

  SmsProvider:
    - send(to, text) -> {success, message_id, provider_status}

Channel Selection:

  priority_routing:
    - critical: push + sms + email
    - high: push + email
    - normal: email
    - low: email digest (batch)

  fallback_chain:
    primary: configured channel
    fallback_1: alternative channel
    fallback_2: email (universal fallback)

Best Practices:

  - Use async processing for email (RabbitMQ) to avoid HTTP request blocking
  - Implement circuit breaker for provider APIs
  - Cache template configs in Redis for performance
  - Use HTML sanitization for user-generated content in templates
  - Track delivery status per provider (Mailgun, Slack)
  - Implement rate limiting per provider to respect API limits
  - Use queue-based retry for failed notifications (not inline retry)

Anti-Patterns:

  - Hardcoded message strings in handlers
  - Synchronous email sending in request path
  - Missing daily limits (notification spam)
  - No retry on transient provider failures
  - Tight coupling to specific provider SDKs
  - Ignoring provider rate limits (getting blocked)

State: +
